{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "design-azure-sql-schema",
  "domain": "schema-and-orm",
  "value": [
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption",
    "multi-step-with-silent-failures"
  ],
  "correction": "Asked to design a schema for Azure SQL Database, an agent sizes string columns in characters, takes the default collation without noticing what it decides, and treats an identity column as a counter that increments by one. Every failure that follows is deferred rather than immediate, which is why review does not catch them. Measured on 2026-08-28 against a live engine reporting EngineEdition 5 and Edition 'SQL Azure': a unique index on NVARCHAR(1000) is created with message 1945 at severity 10, a warning most clients never surface, and the first genuinely long value fails months later with Msg 1946, because the nonclustered key limit of 1700 bytes and the clustered limit of 900 bytes are byte counts and Unicode spends two per character, making NVARCHAR(850) and NVARCHAR(450) the real ceilings. The default collation SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS is case-insensitive, so a table of slugs or API keys rejects 'Ab1X' beside 'aB1x' with Msg 2627, and it is also a SQL collation, which is the one case where a VARCHAR column compared to the Unicode parameter every mainstream driver binds loses its index seek: measured 80 logical reads against 2 over 20,000 rows, with CONVERT_IMPLICIT applied to the column inside an Index Scan, while the identical column under a Windows or UTF-8 collation seeks. Identity is wrong in the same deferred way. The remembered remedy, trace flag 272, is rejected here with Msg 40518, and measured across a single engine restart an INT IDENTITY jumped from 3 to 1002, a BIGINT IDENTITY from 3 to 10002 and a default-cached SEQUENCE from 2 to 51, so any invoice or ticket number built on a bare identity column develops thousand-wide holes on routine reconfiguration, silently and unrecoverably, unless the design uses SEQUENCE ... NO CACHE or ALTER DATABASE SCOPED CONFIGURATION SET IDENTITY_CACHE = OFF.",
    "value_probe": {
      "gate": "The answer finds the three deferred failures before any of them happens, being an index key limit counted in bytes rather than characters, a default collation that ignores case under a key the application treats as case sensitive, and a numbering source that develops large gaps on its own, and it changes the definition rather than only describing the risks.",
      "task": "I am about to add a table on Azure SQL Database and I would like a review before I run it in production.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE dbo.api_client (\n    invoice_no INT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,\n    client_slug NVARCHAR(1000) NOT NULL,\n    created_at DATETIME2 NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_api_client_slug ON dbo.api_client (client_slug);\n```\n\nThe slug is generated by our application and it is case sensitive by design, so 'Ab1X' and 'aB1x' are two different clients and both have to be able to exist. The invoice number is printed on documents customers keep, and our finance team expects the numbers to run consecutively with no holes.\n\nThe database was created with the portal defaults and nobody changed anything about it. I ran this against a test database and it completed with no error at all. What is going to go wrong later, and what should I change before this goes live?",
      "states": [
        "The index key limit is counted in bytes and a Unicode character costs two of them, so the declared length is about double what the index can actually hold.",
        "The index statement is accepted now and the failure appears only when someone inserts a value long enough to exceed the limit, which may be months later.",
        "The default collation ignores case, so two slugs differing only in case collide on the unique index and the second one is rejected.",
        "Identity values are handed out from a cache, so a restart or a reconfiguration of the service leaves a gap of hundreds or thousands with no rows deleted and nothing to recover.",
        "A consecutive number needs a source that is explicitly not cached, or the caching turned off at the database, rather than a plain identity column."
      ],
      "refutes": [
        "The index key limit is a character count, so a 1000 character Unicode column is comfortably inside it and the unique index is fine as written.",
        "The default collation on a new Azure SQL Database distinguishes case, so two slugs differing only in case will be stored as two separate rows.",
        "An identity column produces consecutive values, so the invoice numbers cannot develop gaps unless rows are deleted or a transaction is rolled back.",
        "A server level trace flag can be enabled on Azure SQL Database to stop the identity column skipping values.",
        "The statement completing with no error on the test database means the index definition is valid and there is nothing further to check about its width."
      ]
    },
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "inspect"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db",
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "related": [
    "ef-core-azure-sql",
    "prisma-azure-sql",
    "sqlalchemy-azure-sql",
    "t-sql-correctness",
    "t-sql-json-and-openjson",
    "vector-search-azure-sql",
    "schema-migrations-safely",
    "sql-database-projects",
    "provision-azure-sql-db",
    "diagnose-slow-query",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a database created with no explicit collation, so it carries the default",
      "an administrative connection able to create databases, tables and indexes",
      "the ability to restart or fail over the engine, for the identity cache assertions"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "no CREATE INDEX or CREATE TABLE in the generated schema emits message 1945, so every key column total is at or under 1700 bytes nonclustered and 900 bytes clustered",
      "every string column participating in an index key, a unique constraint or a foreign key carries an explicit length rather than a mapper default",
      "the database collation is stated in CREATE DATABASE, or a comment records that the default was chosen deliberately",
      "every column holding a case-significant token, code or key carries an explicit case-sensitive collation",
      "no table combines a VARCHAR column on a SQL collation with an application that binds Unicode string parameters, and a plan taken for one such lookup shows an Index Seek rather than an Index Scan with CONVERT_IMPLICIT on the column",
      "no human-facing sequential number is generated by a bare IDENTITY column",
      "every CREATE SEQUENCE states START WITH",
      "every nullable unique column uses a filtered unique index, or the design states that one NULL row is the intent",
      "no json or vector column appears in a key, a GROUP BY, a DISTINCT or an equality predicate",
      "no foreign key names a second database"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_keylen (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, email NVARCHAR(1000) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_keylen ON dbo.probe_keylen (email);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_keylen (email) VALUES (REPLICATE(CAST(N'a' AS NVARCHAR(MAX)), 900));",
        "expect": {
          "error": 1946
        },
        "why": "The measurement the whole skill turns on, made executable. The CREATE UNIQUE INDEX on 2000 bytes of key succeeds and emits only message 1945 at severity 10, which most clients never surface, so this batch reaches the INSERT. The 900 character value is 1800 bytes and is refused with Msg 1946. Measured 2026-08-28. The failure arriving on the row rather than on the DDL is the point."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_keywarn (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, email NVARCHAR(1000) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_keywarn ON dbo.probe_keywarn (email);",
        "expect": {
          "warning": 1945
        },
        "why": "The other half of the pair above, and the half nobody sees. The same DDL that sets up Msg 1946 is asserted here on its own: the index over a 2000 byte key is CREATED, the statement succeeds, and the only notice given is message 1945 at severity 10. Measured 2026-08-28, text \"Warning! The maximum key length for a nonclustered index is 1700 bytes\", which Microsoft Learn quotes verbatim on Index JSON data, and sys.messages gives the id 1945 at severity 10. Asserting it needs sqlcmd -m-1, because without that a severity 10 message prints no number and -b never exits non-zero below severity 11, which is exactly why a migration emitting this reports success. Left unasserted, only the deferred error was measured and the moment the mistake is made was not."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_keysum (a NVARCHAR(300) NOT NULL, b NVARCHAR(300) NOT NULL, c NVARCHAR(300) NOT NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_keysum ON dbo.probe_keysum (a, b, c);",
        "expect": {
          "warning": 1945
        },
        "why": "A composite key spends the SUM of its columns, which section 1 states and nothing measured. No column here is anywhere near the limit at 600 bytes each, and the three together are 1800 bytes, so the same message 1945 is emitted and the index is still created. Measured 2026-08-28. This is the shape a real design reaches the warning through, because each column looks harmless on its own and only the key is over budget."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ceiling (email NVARCHAR(850) NOT NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_ceiling ON dbo.probe_ceiling (email);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ceiling (email) VALUES (REPLICATE(CAST(N'a' AS NVARCHAR(MAX)), 850));\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS rows_accepted FROM dbo.probe_ceiling;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The other half of the same claim: NVARCHAR(850) is 1700 bytes exactly, so it is the widest Unicode column a nonclustered key can carry, and a value using every one of those characters is accepted rather than merely tolerated. Measured 2026-08-28, where 851 raised message 1945 and 850 was clean. Without this probe the table of ceilings in section 1 is only half tested."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_inc (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, k NVARCHAR(400) NOT NULL, blob NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_inc ON dbo.probe_inc (k) INCLUDE (blob);",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "INCLUDE columns do not count against the 1700 byte key budget, and NVARCHAR(MAX) is legal there even though it can never be a key column. Measured 2026-08-28. This is the escape hatch the skill offers whenever a design wants a wide column covered, so it is worth proving rather than asserting."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lob (id INT NOT NULL, big NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL);\nCREATE INDEX ix_probe_lob ON dbo.probe_lob (big);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 1919
        },
        "why": "NVARCHAR(MAX) cannot be an index key column at all, which is a different refusal from being over the byte budget: it is rejected at create time rather than warned about. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 1919, column is of a type that is invalid for use as a key column in an index."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_json (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, payload JSON NULL);\nCREATE INDEX ix_probe_json ON dbo.probe_json (payload);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 1978
        },
        "why": "A json column is a storage decision, not a key decision, and the engine says so at create time. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 1978, invalid for use as a key column in an index or statistics. The distinct number from 1919 is why section 6 names both."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_json_eq (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, payload JSON NULL);\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_json_eq WHERE payload = N'{\"a\":1}';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 402
        },
        "why": "A json column cannot be compared with equality against a string, so any design that treats the document as a filterable value fails at the first query rather than at the schema review. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 402, the data types json and nvarchar are incompatible in the equal to operator."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_doc (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, payload JSON NULL);\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_doc ADD tenant AS CAST(JSON_VALUE(payload, '$.tenant') AS NVARCHAR(64)) PERSISTED;\nCREATE INDEX ix_probe_doc_tenant ON dbo.probe_doc (tenant);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_doc (payload) VALUES (N'{\"tenant\":\"acme\"}');\nSELECT tenant FROM dbo.probe_doc;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              "acme"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The remedy the skill offers for the previous probe, run end to end: promote the value out of the document into a PERSISTED computed column and index that. Measured 2026-08-28. Asserting the promoted value rather than plain success proves the computed column is actually populated from the document and not merely accepted by the parser."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_tok_ci (t NVARCHAR(64) COLLATE SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_tok_ci (t) VALUES (N'aB1x');\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_tok_ci (t) VALUES (N'Ab1X');",
        "expect": {
          "error": 2627
        },
        "why": "The default collation of a new database is SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS, and CI means two case-different tokens are the same key. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 2627. The collation is stated on the column rather than inherited so the probe measures case insensitivity itself and not whatever collation the scratch database happens to carry."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_tok_cs (t NVARCHAR(64) COLLATE Latin1_General_CS_AS NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_tok_cs (t) VALUES (N'aB1x'), (N'Ab1X');\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS rows_accepted FROM dbo.probe_tok_cs;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              2
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The fix for the previous probe, and the reason the skill says the fix is per column rather than per database: the same two values are two distinct keys under an explicit case-sensitive collation. Measured 2026-08-28, both rows accepted."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_null (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, code NVARCHAR(64) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_null ON dbo.probe_null (code);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_null (code) VALUES (NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_null (code) VALUES (NULL);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 2601
        },
        "why": "A unique index here treats NULL as a value, so it accepts exactly one NULL row and refuses the second. This is the belief an agent carries over from PostgreSQL, where every NULL is distinct. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 2601, the duplicate key value is (<NULL>)."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_null_f (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, code NVARCHAR(64) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_probe_null_f ON dbo.probe_null_f (code) WHERE code IS NOT NULL;\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_null_f (code) VALUES (NULL), (NULL), (N'x');\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS rows_accepted FROM dbo.probe_null_f;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              3
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The filtered unique index the skill prescribes, proved to accept many NULL rows while still enforcing uniqueness on the values that are present. Measured 2026-08-28. It also exercises the QUOTED_IDENTIFIER requirement: a session without it fails to create this index with Msg 1934, so a green result says the runner's session is set correctly too."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DBCC TRACEON (272, -1);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40518
        },
        "why": "The remedy an agent recalls for identity gaps from a non-PaaS engine, and the measured proof it does not exist here. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 40518. Microsoft Learn lists 40518 as \"DBCC command '%.*ls' is not supported in this version of SQL Server\" and lists trace flags among the T-SQL not supported in Azure SQL Database, so the number and the refusal are both sourced."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_fk_child (id INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, parent_id INT NOT NULL);\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_fk_child ADD CONSTRAINT fk_probe_x FOREIGN KEY (parent_id)\n  REFERENCES otherdb.dbo.some_parent (id);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40515
        },
        "why": "A foreign key cannot reach another database, so a shared-lookup-database design has no referential integrity here. Measured 2026-08-28 with both databases present on the same server, which is why this is the database boundary and not a missing object. Microsoft Learn gives 40515 as \"Reference to database and/or server name in '%.*ls' is not supported in this version of SQL Server\"."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_p (k NVARCHAR(64) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c (id INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, k NVARCHAR(128) NOT NULL);\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_c ADD CONSTRAINT fk_probe_len FOREIGN KEY (k) REFERENCES dbo.probe_p (k);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 1753
        },
        "why": "A child key column must match the parent's length exactly, which is how an oversized parent key propagates through a schema instead of staying local. Measured 2026-08-28, Msg 1753, columns participating in a foreign key relationship must be defined with the same length and scale."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE SEQUENCE dbo.probe_seq_nostart AS BIGINT;\nSELECT CASE WHEN CAST(start_value AS BIGINT) = CAST(minimum_value AS BIGINT) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS starts_at_type_minimum\nFROM sys.sequences WHERE name = 'probe_seq_nostart';",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "A CREATE SEQUENCE with no START WITH does not start at 1, it starts at the minimum of its type, which for BIGINT is -9223372036854775808. Measured 2026-08-28. The comparison is made against the catalog's own minimum_value rather than a literal, because the literal -9223372036854775808 is parsed as numeric rather than bigint and would make the probe about T-SQL literal typing instead."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_heap (id INT NOT NULL, note NVARCHAR(20) NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_heap (id, note) VALUES (1, N'a'), (2, N'b');\nSELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_heap) AS rows_accepted,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.indexes WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID('dbo.probe_heap') AND type = 1) AS clustered_indexes;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              2,
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The correction in the Do not list, made executable: a table with no clustered index is created and takes rows. The belief that this engine requires a clustered index on every table is out of date. Measured 2026-08-28. Two counts are asserted together so the probe cannot pass on a table that quietly acquired a clustered index from a primary key."
      }
    ,
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_ci (s nvarchar(20) COLLATE Latin1_General_CI_AS);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_bin (s nvarchar(20) COLLATE Latin1_General_BIN2);\nGO\nSELECT a.s FROM dbo.probe_d_ci AS a JOIN dbo.probe_d_bin AS b ON a.s = b.s;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 468
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A join across two collations is refused rather than resolved, which is the second half of what the database default costs. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_p20 (k nvarchar(20) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_c50 (k nvarchar(50) NOT NULL, CONSTRAINT fk_probe_d_len FOREIGN KEY (k) REFERENCES dbo.probe_d_p20(k));",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1753
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A foreign key column must match the parent's length exactly, so an oversized parent key propagates to every child rather than being narrowed at the child. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_pc (k nvarchar(20) COLLATE Latin1_General_CI_AS NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_cc (k nvarchar(20) COLLATE Latin1_General_BIN2 NOT NULL, CONSTRAINT fk_probe_d_coll FOREIGN KEY (k) REFERENCES dbo.probe_d_pc(k));",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1757
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The same rule for collation, which is the one that bites when a single column was given a collation by hand. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_nvmax (s nvarchar(max) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1919
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. nvarchar(max) cannot be a key column. Note the number: this is 1919 and the json and vector cases below are 1978, which is why the skill prints both. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_jkey (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, payload json);\nGO\nCREATE INDEX probe_d_jkey_ix ON dbo.probe_d_jkey (payload);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1978
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A json column cannot be an index key, the row this skill's storage table asserts. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_vkey (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, v vector(3));\nGO\nCREATE INDEX probe_d_vkey_ix ON dbo.probe_d_vkey (v);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1978
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. And neither can a vector column, the other half of the same row. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_dim (v vector(1999));",
            "expect": {
              "error": 2717
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The 1998 dimension ceiling, asserted here as well as in vector-search-azure-sql because this skill is where the choice of column type is made. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "DBCC TRACEON (272, -1);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 40518
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. Trace flag 272, the identity cache lever every answer from a boxed engine reaches for, is not available here, which is why the skill routes to a sequence instead. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "SELECT * FROM probe_d_otherdb.dbo.some_parent;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 40515
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A three part name across databases is refused, which is what makes a cross database foreign key impossible rather than merely discouraged. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_uidx (id int IDENTITY, s nvarchar(20) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX probe_d_uidx_ix ON dbo.probe_d_uidx (s);\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_d_uidx (s) VALUES (NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_d_uidx (s) VALUES (NULL);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 2601
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A unique index treats two NULLs as duplicates, which is the behaviour that makes an optional unique field unusable without a filtered index. The number differs from the constraint case below, and the skill says so. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_ucon (s nvarchar(20) NOT NULL CONSTRAINT uq_probe_d UNIQUE);\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_d_ucon (s) VALUES (N'a');\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_d_ucon (s) VALUES (N'a');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 2627
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The same collision through a unique constraint reports a different number, which is the distinction the skill draws so a log line can be read back to its cause. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_d_filt (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, slug nvarchar(50) NULL);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX probe_d_filt_ix ON dbo.probe_d_filt (slug) WHERE slug IS NOT NULL;\nGO\nSET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER OFF;\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_d_filt (id, slug) VALUES (1, N'a');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1934
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The filtered index is the fix for the NULL problem above, and it puts a requirement on every session that writes to the table. Drivers set QUOTED_IDENTIFIER; a command line client may not, which is how this reaches production as an intermittent write failure. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          }
    ]
  },
